WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.360
Hello there! Perhaps your&nbsp;
academic social media exploded too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.320
or maybe you are still unaware&nbsp;
of the news, but it is true!&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:21.120
A pure Transformer beats the state-of-the art in&nbsp;
image recognition! Yes, you heard right! Images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:28.080
vision, the realm dominated for centuries by the&nbsp;
Convolutional Neural Networks, or short CNNs.&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.240
In this video we will explain how&nbsp;
the Transformer was able to beat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.200
the CNN on its own image battleground
and we will discuss whether the rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.600
of the Transformer will really&nbsp;
mean the extinction of CNNs.&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.000
So it happened. The Transformers, which we&nbsp;
explained in a previous video, linked below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.160
took over the field of natural language&nbsp;
processing during the last couple of years.&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.960
It recently moved on to integrate vision and&nbsp;
language in Multimodal Transformer architectures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.320
(find the explanation linked below).
Now, after some CNN-Transformer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.200
hybrid attempts it happened:
The pure Vision Transformer is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.720
the new state of the art, and this ruins&nbsp;
the CNN fascination of Ms. Coffee Bean!&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.520
Or... oh really?
But how does the transformer do it?&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.400
Well, by doing it better than CNNs for a start...!
A convolutional neural network uses kernels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.520
to aggregate very local information&nbsp;
in each layer, which then is passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.120
to the next layer that aggregates again&nbsp;
local information, but now with a larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.640
field of view because it looks at information&nbsp;
that was already aggregated by the first layer.&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.240
The take home message here is that CNNs&nbsp;
start to look very locally and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.840
receptive field becomes more global in each&nbsp;
layer and after quite some training time.&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:02.720
But the Vision Transformer presented in this VERY&nbsp;
anonymous paper succeeds at having a large field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.480
of view from the very beginning. It overcomes&nbsp;
the limitations of CNNs that look very narrowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.240
at first (limitations which helps when there's&nbsp;
not much training data, but about that later).&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.360
The Transformer looks at the image by&nbsp;
taking the input image and splitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.920
it into patches of 16 by 16 pixels.
These patches are then flattened by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.160
linear transformation, a matrix,&nbsp;
so that they become vectors.&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.200
Then, everything else what happens we know&nbsp;
from the language Transformer, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.960
the same thing is applied here: The vector of&nbsp;
each patch gets positional embeddings and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.120
usual Transformer is then used to predict what the&nbsp;
image might represent. Done. This was the method.&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:55.120
Why does it do better than CNNs? Because the&nbsp;
"anonymous" authors can train on an awful lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:02.080
of training data for an awful lot of TPU days.
That's something all anonymous authors can do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.760
Or not.
The Transformer is (unlike the CNN) very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:14.480
data-hungry, especially because it has the freedom&nbsp;
to look everywhere onto the image from the start.&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.920
In other words, it is very unfocused&nbsp;
at first and it needs huge data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.200
amounts to learn how to focus and what to focus&nbsp;
its attention on to make right predictions.&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:32.000
On many amounts of data, the Transformer can&nbsp;
find very original and unexpected ways to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.040
at the data, because there is&nbsp;
no element in the architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:41.920
that tells the model how to do that exactly.
The CNN however, is focused from the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:48.240
by the convolutions towards a local view.
The given focus patterns can be a limitation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.600
but we can also spare a lot of training data&nbsp;
on this, because we do not have to teach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:01.200
model how to focus, only what to focus.
So will Transformers replace CNNs?&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:09.440
For heavyweight state-of-the art winners,&nbsp;
benchmark crushing, GPU intensive, data-hungry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:16.960
energy consuming, environmental unfriendly&nbsp;
kind of AI, yes, most probably, yeah possibly.&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.200
For lightweight Machine Learning in production,&nbsp;
for computational and energy economic settings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.400
for Green AI…. CNNs and even&nbsp;
far more classical approaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:35.120
will still be out there, at least for a while.
The paper discussed here is at ICLR still under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:41.520
double-blind review. We will update the video&nbsp;
description with the full paper information, after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.680
the authors have been revealed. Until then, try&nbsp;
to figure out yourself who the authors might be!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:54.080
But do not tell the paper’s reviewers, otherwise&nbsp;
you might ruin the double-blind peer review!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:09.840
Or was the whole setting ruined already?
Ok, bye!

